<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aratta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aratta is a distant land rich in precious stones and metals described in Sumerian epic poems, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmerkar">Enmerkar</a> and the Lord of Aratta, in which Enmerkar, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, demands that its lord send lapis lazuli and craftsmen to adorn the temples of Uruk. Its location, somewhere on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>, is unknown, and it may be partly or wholly legendary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aratta.jpg" alt="Aratta" width="122" height="109" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laszlovszky András at Hungarian Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aratta_sumer_%C3%ADr%C3%A1sjel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Aratta appears in the Sumerian tales of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmerkar">Enmerkar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugalbanda">Lugalbanda</a>, two early and possibly mythical kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> who are also named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>. The poems describe it as fabulously rich in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and lapis lazuli and in the artisans who worked them, remote and hard to reach, and the home of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> until she transferred her favour to Uruk. In Enmerkar and the Lord of Aratta a messenger crosses the Zubi mountains, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> and five, six, seven mountains before reaching it, and Aratta asks for grain in return for its treasures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmerkar">Enmerkar</a> and En-suhgir-ana the lord of Aratta challenges Enmerkar for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a>'s affection and sends a sorcerer from Hamazi to bewitch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>'s livestock at Eresh, but a wise woman defeats him and casts him into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, and the lord submits. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugalbanda">Lugalbanda</a> poems Enmerkar's army marches through the mountains against a rebellious Aratta, and Inanna advises him to carry off its metalsmiths, stonemasons and moulds; the city is given battlements of green lapis lazuli. Later hymns, proverbs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> cylinder name Aratta as a byword for treasure and remoteness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Early 20th-century scholars took Aratta for an epithet of Shuruppak, a view since abandoned. In 1963 Samuel Noah Kramer placed it near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a>, reading a Mount Hurum in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugalbanda">Lugalbanda</a> poem as a reference to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrians">Hurrians</a>, but the phrase is now read as mountain cave. Others looked to the Great Khorasan Road through northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> was identified with Tall-i Malyan in 1973, it lay 600 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, far from those routes. Yousef Majidzadeh has proposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiroft">Jiroft</a> culture of eastern Iran. No archaeological trace of Aratta has been found, and Hansman warned in 1978 against over-speculation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aratta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sumerian poems tell of Gilgamesh resisting Aga of Kish, and of Enmerkar demanding precious stone from the distant land of <b>Aratta</b>; these are literary works composed centuries later, and how far they reflect real events is uncertain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Military</a></li></ul></section>
